1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:05,505
This is the water under one platform in the US Gulf.

2
00:00:05,505 --> 00:00:07,207
2,388 metres of it.

3
00:00:07,716 --> 00:00:09,064
And below the seabed:

4
00:00:09,064 --> 00:00:14,457
80 wellbores, almost all of them drawn from survey records that anyone can download.

5
00:00:15,216 --> 00:00:17,175
This is the Shell Asset Atlas.

6
00:00:17,175 --> 00:00:22,661
An unofficial project, with no connection to Shell, built entirely from public data.

7
00:00:24,516 --> 00:00:28,824
Operators report what they produce to public regulators, every month.

8
00:00:28,824 --> 00:00:31,321
The atlas puts those records on a globe.

9
00:00:31,849 --> 00:00:34,156
210 installations and sites.

10
00:00:34,156 --> 00:00:35,969
48 oil and gas fields.

11
00:00:35,969 --> 00:00:38,852
About 5,900 kilometres of pipeline.

12
00:00:38,852 --> 00:00:44,948
Drawn from regulators in the United States, the United Kingdom and Norway.

13
00:00:45,550 --> 00:00:46,877
Start in the US Gulf.

14
00:00:46,877 --> 00:00:48,899
Each column is a production hub,

15
00:00:48,899 --> 00:00:52,185
and its height is what that hub produced in a month.

16
00:00:52,650 --> 00:00:54,866
Click one for its record.

17
00:00:54,866 --> 00:00:56,019
This is Mars:

18
00:00:56,019 --> 00:01:01,960
a tension-leg platform, standing in 894 metres of water since 1996,

19
00:01:01,960 --> 00:01:05,685
with every month of production since 2015.

20
00:01:07,350 --> 00:01:08,291
Now press play.

21
00:01:08,291 --> 00:01:11,240
A decade of production replays, month by month,

22
00:01:11,240 --> 00:01:13,750
and the leaderboard re-ranks as it goes.

23
00:01:14,316 --> 00:01:20,004
By December 2025, the total is about a million barrels of oil equivalent a day,

24
00:01:20,004 --> 00:01:20,436
gross.

25
00:01:20,436 --> 00:01:22,740
But look at the dips on the way.

26
00:01:22,740 --> 00:01:24,684
Several of them are storms.

27
00:01:28,116 --> 00:01:32,808
The atlas follows 26 Gulf storms that passed near Shell structures.

28
00:01:32,808 --> 00:01:35,049
The costliest was Hurricane Ida.

29
00:01:35,583 --> 00:01:38,738
Click it, and the atlas goes back to July 2021.

30
00:01:38,738 --> 00:01:42,766
Ida crosses the Gulf in August as a category four hurricane,

31
00:01:42,766 --> 00:01:47,466
and its centre passes two kilometres from the nearest Shell structure.

32
00:01:47,916 --> 00:01:53,158
The month after, Shell-operated production in the Gulf falls by more than half.

33
00:01:53,158 --> 00:01:58,268
Mars goes from about 182 thousand barrels of oil equivalent a day to nothing,

34
00:01:58,268 --> 00:02:00,524
and takes three months to recover.

35
00:02:01,083 --> 00:02:02,121
Across two hubs,

36
00:02:02,121 --> 00:02:06,795
the atlas estimates about 10 million barrels of oil equivalent deferred,

37
00:02:06,795 --> 00:02:09,067
relative to the pre-storm baseline.

38
00:02:09,067 --> 00:02:11,664
It's an estimate, and the panel says so.

39
00:02:12,216 --> 00:02:16,235
Each week, it also checks the National Hurricane Center's feed.

40
00:02:16,235 --> 00:02:18,723
At the last check, no storm was active.

41
00:02:19,349 --> 00:02:22,819
The regulator publishes every operator, not only Shell.

42
00:02:22,819 --> 00:02:25,216
Press B, and the atlas ranks them all.

43
00:02:25,683 --> 00:02:28,544
Go back to 2015 and play the decade again.

44
00:02:28,544 --> 00:02:33,448
This time the bars are companies, ranked by the production they operate.

45
00:02:39,583 --> 00:02:43,437
Stop at September 2021, the month after Ida.

46
00:02:43,437 --> 00:02:45,364
BP has taken the lead.

47
00:02:45,916 --> 00:02:52,258
By December 2025, Shell ranks number one of 30 operators in the US Gulf,

48
00:02:52,258 --> 00:02:56,222
with about 28 percent of operated production.

49
00:02:56,222 --> 00:02:59,569
It has led in 123 of these 132 months.

50
00:03:00,150 --> 00:03:01,874
It isn't first everywhere.

51
00:03:01,874 --> 00:03:05,521
In Norway, Shell ranks number five, far behind Equinor.

52
00:03:05,521 --> 00:03:09,302
And this is production by operator of record, not equity.

53
00:03:10,383 --> 00:03:12,977
Now, the place this film began.

54
00:03:12,977 --> 00:03:14,567
Search for Perdido:

55
00:03:14,567 --> 00:03:17,914
a spar, moored in 2,388 metres of water.

56
00:03:18,383 --> 00:03:20,382
Its record has one more button.

57
00:03:20,382 --> 00:03:21,092
Dive below.

58
00:03:21,550 --> 00:03:24,317
Everything here is drawn to true scale.

59
00:03:24,317 --> 00:03:28,291
The water is as deep as 2.9 Burj Khalifas, stood on end.

60
00:03:28,750 --> 00:03:31,782
Then the seabed, and below it, 80 wellbores.

61
00:03:31,782 --> 00:03:36,399
76 of them follow the directional surveys filed with the regulator.

62
00:03:36,399 --> 00:03:39,225
The rest are drawn dashed, as schematics.

63
00:03:39,816 --> 00:03:44,056
The deepest ends 6,337 metres below sea level.

64
00:03:44,056 --> 00:03:49,587
Together, they come to about 157 kilometres of drilled hole.

65
00:03:50,150 --> 00:03:53,969
Stretch the depth three times, and each path is easier to follow.

66
00:03:56,050 --> 00:04:01,673
Colour them by the year drilling began, and the field grows in front of you,

67
00:04:01,673 --> 00:04:03,005
from 2002 to 2025.

68
00:04:03,583 --> 00:04:04,811
Drag to look around.

69
00:04:04,811 --> 00:04:08,618
Then point at any well for its name, its depth and its status.

70
00:04:11,483 --> 00:04:13,838
The atlas draws only what is public.

71
00:04:13,838 --> 00:04:18,678
The seabed is interpolated from well-site depths, and no geology is shown,

72
00:04:18,678 --> 00:04:20,379
because none is published.

73
00:04:22,216 --> 00:04:25,996
Back on the surface, Troll, off Norway, tops the leaderboard.

74
00:04:25,996 --> 00:04:27,792
But Shell doesn't operate it.

75
00:04:27,792 --> 00:04:29,341
It holds about 8 percent.

76
00:04:29,850 --> 00:04:33,489
So the atlas shows Shell's net figure beside the gross one.

77
00:04:33,489 --> 00:04:35,525
Gross is never passed off as net.

78
00:04:36,650 --> 00:04:40,394
Beyond production, 26 more sites are placed by hand,

79
00:04:40,394 --> 00:04:44,138
from LNG plants and refineries to hydrogen and wind.

80
00:04:44,650 --> 00:04:45,867
And on the street,

81
00:04:45,867 --> 00:04:51,751
OpenStreetMap volunteers have mapped more than 28 thousand Shell-branded fuel stations.

82
00:04:51,751 --> 00:04:54,186
One click turns them into a skyline.

83
00:04:55,350 --> 00:04:57,576
None of it has to be taken on trust.

84
00:04:57,576 --> 00:05:00,854
Data and method lists every source, with its licence.

85
00:05:01,383 --> 00:05:04,647
A pipeline rebuilds the atlas from them every week.

86
00:05:04,647 --> 00:05:09,767
If a regulator's site is down, it falls back to the last good copy, and says so.

87
00:05:14,416 --> 00:05:15,798
The Shell Asset Atlas.

88
00:05:15,798 --> 00:05:17,619
Public records, on one globe,

89
00:05:17,619 --> 00:05:21,072
from the sea surface to the bottom of the deepest well.

90
00:05:22,149 --> 00:05:26,076
It's an unofficial project, and it isn't endorsed by Shell.

91
00:05:26,076 --> 00:05:30,868
The data belongs to the regulators, and to OpenStreetMap's contributors.

92
00:05:30,868 --> 00:05:33,264
This narration is a synthetic voice.
